-- title:
--   Weak compactness in canonically reflexive spaces
-- statement:
--   These notions belong to a proof that every reflexive real Banach space has the fixed point property for nonexpansive maps: every $F:C\to C$ with $\|Fa-Fb\|\le\|a-b\|$ on a nonempty closed bounded convex set $C$ has a fixed point. The proof assumes a fixed-point-free map, reduces to a minimal invariant set $K$ of diameter $1$, builds an adaptive weighted tree of nonexpansive maps generated by resolvents, and contradicts the minimality through detecting functionals. This module collects the weak-topology facts used throughout; it introduces no new data beyond the inverse of the canonical bidual map.
--
--   1. **Separation.** Continuous linear functionals separate the points of the weak space, which is therefore Hausdorff.
--   2. **Closed convex sets.** By Hahn–Banach separation, norm-closed convex sets are weakly closed.
--   3. **Balls.** If $X\to X^{**}$ is onto, its inverse is weak-star-to-weak continuous, since its scalar evaluations are bidual evaluations. By Banach–Alaoglu every closed ball is weakly compact, and hence every closed bounded convex set
--   $$
--   C\subseteq X\ \text{ is weakly compact}.
--   $$
